WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 81, it's May 26th, 2026.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.080
Let's look at what's new across the frontiers of space and science.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:16.560
NASA's psyche spacecraft is using Mars gravity to reach a metal asteroid never visited before.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.180
The probe used the planet's gravity to adjust its trajectory toward the metal asteroid.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.100
This maneuver shortens the journey and conserves onboard fuel.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.960
Psychie's target is a body thought to be the exposed core of an early planetesimal.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:35.080
Arrival is still years away after the flyby.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.300
Scientists expect the asteroid to reveal the interior structure of a planetesimal

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.380
that never fully formed into a planet.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.040
Data collected after arrival could reshape models of how planetary cores differentiate.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:55.580
While psyche looks back at solar system origins, another Mars mission is adding fresh detail to the planet's watery past.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:02.080
The Perseverance rover identified rock formations indicating past water flows in JZero Crater.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.360
These features include layered sediments and mineral deposits consistent with prolonged liquid activity.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.680
The observations add detail to the timeline of when Mars held surface water.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.320
Follow-up analysis of returned samples could confirm the duration of habitable conditions.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.160
Understanding past water on Mars also sharpens how we interpret marginal conditions

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:26.740
on distant exoplanets.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:32.540
The James Webb Telescope observed atmospheric changes on a world 700 light years away.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.820
Data revealed temperature variations and cloud patterns across the planet's surface.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:42.440
This marks one of the first detailed weather studies of an exoplanet.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.560
Continued monitoring will track seasonal shifts over multiple orbits.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.340
Those same atmospheric processes face an unexpected threat from the centers of galaxies.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:59.240
Models show radiation from distant black holes can erode atmospheres on planets far from their stars.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:04.800
The effect extends habitability concerns beyond the traditional Goldilocks zone.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.860
Planets and otherwise stable orbits may lose protective gases over time.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.280
Researchers are mapping which galactic regions pose the greatest risk.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:20.800
A supermassive black hole at the center of a galaxy can outshine its entire stellar population,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.020
flooding surrounding space with intense radiation.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.960
If that energy reaches an exoplanet hundreds of light years away,

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:34.000
it can heat the upper atmosphere enough for gas molecules to reach escape velocity and drift into space.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:42.560
The process unfolds over millions of years, gradually thinning a world that might otherwise sit in a comfortable orbit around its star.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.240
One surprising detail is that the danger zone can extend thousands of light years from the black hole

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.600
far beyond the reach of its gravity.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.720
We still do not know how often this atmospheric stripping actually occurs,

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.540
or which galactic environments leave planets most vulnerable.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.580
While galactic scale forces reshape distant worlds,

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.320
nearer term decisions are reshaping how crews reach low Earth orbit.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:16.120
NASA plans to order up to three missions immediately and three more later under the SpaceX contract.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.600
The decision follows delays in Boeing's starliner certification.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.880
SpaceX will now handle a larger share of crew rotations to the station.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:30.220
The added flights extend through the end of the current contract period.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.740
Reliable crew access also depends on steady cargo logistics.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:40.640
Each company purchased a full Falcon 9 launch to serve multiple small satellite customers.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.540
The move responds to rising demand for dedicated small payload missions.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.500
Both firms have previously arranged rides on shared flights.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.580
The new contracts allow tighter scheduling and orbit customization.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.780
Commercial servicing is another area seeing fresh investment.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:03.600
The partnership will test how orbit fabs docking hardware connects to electric propulsion systems.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:09.520
Thales, Alina's thrusters use xenon or similar propellants that could be replenished in orbit.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.240
The study examines mechanical, fluid, and electrical compatibility.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.680
Results will guide future commercial servicing missions.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.860
Protecting satellites once they are on orbit is equally important.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.660
The system combines tracking data with protective measures for orbital assets.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:32.820
New imagery shows the expanded monitoring network now covering more of the space environment.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:37.840
Officials release the images to demonstrate improved situational awareness.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.640
The project focuses on both collision avoidance and interference detection.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:48.200
Closer to home, engineers are already rehearsing the next surface operations on the moon.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:53.040
Autonomous rovers are undergoing trials in Imperial County Desert terrain.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.500
The Bureau of Land Management provides the test site that mimics lunar surface conditions.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.600
Engineers evaluate navigation and obstacle avoidance without human intervention.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:08.620
Data from these runs will refine designs for future Artemis surface operations.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:14.180
Those surface operations will require careful planning by the people defining the walks.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:20.280
Jacqueline Kagey serves as the Artemis extra vehicular activity lead in the flight operations

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.260
directorate. Her team defines procedures for surface walks at the lunar south pole.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.140
The work includes training astronauts for terrain never explored by humans.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.700
Preparations also support later deep space exploration goals.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:39.940
Routine calibration work keeps the data flowing that makes all these missions possible.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:45.300
The Landsat 9 satellite turns its instruments toward the lunar surface once a month.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:49.320
The full moon provides a stable well-known brightness reference.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:54.520
Engineers compare these readings against previous data to track sensor performance.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:59.060
The routine helps maintain consistent earth observation measurements.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:04.900
The dragon capsule carried roughly 6,500 pounds of experiments and provisions.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:10.100
Regular cargo flights maintain continuous operations aboard the orbiting laboratory.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:15.500
The mission underscores the steady logistics required even during high profile launches.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:19.380
Unloading and experiment activation are now underway.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.020
The route uses gravitational interactions to reduce required propellant.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:29.520
Spacecraft following this path can reach the moon with less delta V than traditional transfers.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:34.280
The discovery could lower costs for both robotic and crude lunar missions.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.300
Further modeling will map the most efficient insertion points.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:43.060
The device will test fluid behavior and materials performance in microgravity.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.920
Results will support development of future spacecraft systems.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.740
The experiment joins a steady stream of investigations reaching the station.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.920
Crew members will activate and monitor the hardware during the mission.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:02.420
Studying the red planet's thin atmosphere and tectonic history helps interpret distant rocky worlds.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:07.820
Many discovered exoplanets sit near the edge of habitability, much like Mars.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:13.500
Data on atmospheric escape and surface processes can be scaled to those targets.

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.160
The approach bridges solar system exploration with exoplanet science.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.740
New instruments and mission extensions keep revealing how interconnected our solar system

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:29.360
and beyond truly are. Keep an eye on the next round of small satellite launches scheduled for the

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:35.560
coming weeks. That covers today's space and science news. Share this with a fellow space enthusiast

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:39.640
if you found it interesting. I'm Patrick in Vancouver. See you tomorrow.

